## What I delivered

**D1 — identity typing (all 19 families).** `subject_id` / `version` / `content_hash` / `schema_version` now carry the brief's exact schema wherever declared; `supersedes`/`invalidates`, `required`, and `additionalProperties` are untouched. `TechnologyAdmission` (which had only `{"type":"string"}`) was tightened to the canonical spec — a pure narrowing, and all 22 live admission records pass it. `PI-PKG.trace` is now `{"type":"array","items":{…trace-link…}}`. I probed the offered `$ref` choice: `urn:pipd:s0:TraceLink:1` raises `Unresolvable` under the harness validator (no registry injected), so a bare `$ref` would break every PI, and I inlined the equivalent object constraint.

**D2 — profile fail-closed.** `compute_profile` now refuses non-list / empty / non-string / duplicated axes as typed `ProfileVeto` (unknown axes stay `UnsupportedSurface`) before any escalation math, and returns one normalised list that `profile_record` consumes verbatim — so the escalation basis and the emitted record can no longer disagree.

**D3 — risk honesty.** `risk_assessment="NOT_ASSESSED"` when no evidence, never `LOW_RISK_VERIFIED`. Since `ProfileBinding.schema.json` does not declare the property, adding it would be a breaking change — so it rides in the existing `_profile_meta` sidecar. `vetoes` stays `[]`: no veto evidence exists.

**validate.py** needed no change — SCHEMA already runs before SEMANTIC and the finding already names contract + pointer (`ArtifactIdentity/subject_id: 12345678 is not of type 'string'`).

